<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bou Craa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bou Kra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bou Craa is a phosphate mine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sahara">Western Sahara</a>, about 100 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laayoune">El Aaiún</a>. The deposits were discovered by Spanish geologists in 1947 and production began in the early 1970s, with ore carried to the coast on a conveyor belt about 100 kilometres long. The mine's wealth made the territory valuable to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, and since 1975 it has been operated under Moroccan control.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bou-craa.jpg" alt="Bou Craa" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Earth Science and Remote Sensing Unit, NASA Johnson Space Center · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ISS056-E-32453_-_View_of_Western_Sahara.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
